{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nDuring a local `make verify` (via `.rebar/scratch/verify-slots/verify-slot.sh`, up to 3 concurrent runs on the host) at commit `e2795a076d1` (worktree `bluewhale-llm-proxy`, 2026-11-22 13:38-15:11 PST), 2 unit tests in unrelated modules hit the 300 s pytest-timeout and crashed their xdist workers. Both were blocked in the same place: a `git` subprocess started by `rebar init`'s config ensures never returned.\n\n- `tests/unit/test_attestation_signing.py::test_sign_manifest_without_kind_omits_field` (worker gw1): `store` fixture (line 41) -> `init_repo` -> `init_core` -> `_run_ensures_logged` -> `run_ensures` -> `_init_ensures._gc_config_unit` (line 70) -> `_git` -> `run_git_write` -> `_with_index_lock_retry` -> `_with_transient_fault_retry` -> `run_git_bounded` -> `run_git` -> `_run_in_group` (gitutil.py:309) -> `subprocess.communicate` waiting in `selectors.select`.\n- `tests/unit/test_compact_sweep_perf.py::test_ledger_is_identical_with_and_without_a_prebuilt_map` (worker gw2): same chain, but from `_init_ensures._merge_ours_unit` (line 84).\n\nThe same suite passed on the parent commit's tree an hour earlier (21766 passed, 51 min). This run took 1 h 30 min (21771 passed, 2 failed), so the host was heavily loaded. Re-running both tests alone (3 times, `-n0`) and both modules under `-n4` passes every time (2/2, 45/45).\n\nA `git config` write should take milliseconds. `run_git_write` is bounded by `_LOCAL_GIT_TIMEOUT` (120 s per the store's own comments), yet these calls were still inside `communicate()` when the 300 s test timeout fired. Either the bound did not apply here, or the retry wrappers re-ran a bounded call enough times to pass 300 s.\n\n## Findings\n- Worst-case wall time of one `run_git_write` call. A timeout (code 124) matches neither retry's signature, so it is not re-run; a held `config.lock` fails at once (measured: exit 255 in 1.2 s, no retry). One stalled call therefore costs: up to 30 s waiting for the per-store git-op lock (`_STORE_GIT_LOCK_WAIT_S`, only when a peer holds it) + the 120 s bound (`_LOCAL_GIT_TIMEOUT`) + up to 10 s of process-group reaping (5 s grace + 5 s drain) = 160 s. Theoretical ceiling if every attempt printed a lock or transient signature just before the bound: about 3,970 s (10 x 3 x 130 s + 30 s + backoff); no observed failure takes that path.\n- The bound held in the failing run: both stacks are inside the bounded `communicate()` (`gitutil.py:309`), not reaping or a retry; the 300 s accumulated across sequential bounded calls.\n- Reproduced deterministically with a `git` shim that stalls every `git config` in the tracker before git runs (a stalled filesystem):\n  - Real 120 s bound, the 2 failing tests under `-n2`: both hit the 300 s pytest-timeout with the stack in `_run_in_group` `communicate()` at `gitutil.py:309`, the failing run's signature. The stalled calls were init's own `_ensure_branch_user_config` (`init._git`), before the ensure sweep.\n  - Bound patched to 2 s: `init_repo` issued 7 stalled config calls in the sweep (14.6 s) and reported `gc-config` and `merge-ours` as `changed` although no write happened, and recorded both in `.ensure-applied`, so `fsck` and the write-path hint count them as applied.\n- Defect: init and the ensure units read a timed-out git (124, empty stdout) as an answer (\"unset\", \"absent\") and go on to issue the next git call, which also stalls. Every sequential call pays its own bound, and the outcome is misreported as converged.\n- The failing run's two stacks (one in `_gc_config_unit` line 70, one in `_merge_ours_unit` line 84) show some calls stalled and others completed, so the host stall was partial, not a constant 120 s per call. What stalled git on the host is not established (no sleep or wake in `pmset -g log` for the window; `git config` alone takes 3 ms with no child processes).\n\n## Approach\nTreat a watchdog-timed-out git in the ensure units as a stop signal, not as an answer. Init's own bootstrap calls are out of scope (an init stopped mid-bootstrap can leave a half-mounted tracker): follow-up bug corrupting-plaster-seagull.\n1. `rebar._store.git_outcome`, which owns `is_watchdog_timeout`, gains `GitTimedOut(RuntimeError)` (with an `operation` attribute) and `raise_if_watchdog_timeout(result, operation)`. Private copies in `fsck_tracker_health` and `git_fetch` stay as they are.\n2. `_init_ensures._git` passes every result through it, so gc-config, merge-ours, gitattributes, gitignore and store-compat stop at their first timed-out call. `_untrack_runtime_markers_unit` (init.py, on `init._git`) does the same for its `ls-files`, `rm` and `commit` results; `init._git` itself is unchanged.\n3. `run_ensures` catches `GitTimedOut`, records that unit `failed` with the command and the count of units not run, and runs no further unit in this sweep. Units not run produce no outcome, so `.ensure-applied` and the `fsck` count do not claim them; the next sweep runs every unit as always. Other exceptions keep record-and-continue. env-id, projects-* and snapshot-close-metadata are unchanged.\n4. `_gc_config_unit` and `_merge_ours_unit` return `failed` with git's stderr when a write fails (e.g. a held `config.lock`), instead of `changed`.\n5. The bootstrap pin in `_mount_or_create_branch` calls a new `_pin_auto_maintenance(repo)`, which runs `_gc_config_unit(repo)` and on `GitTimedOut` logs a warning and returns, so init proceeds as today after 1 bound instead of up to 5. `_mount_or_create_branch` stays at complexity 18.\n6. Docs: `docs/migrations.md` (`failed = raised`, both \"always continues\" statements, the marker's \"non-failed ids\" line) and in `ensures.py` the marker comment and the module, `EnsureOutcome` and `run_ensures` docstrings, plus `init._run_ensures_logged`'s docstring.\nEffect: a stalled `git config` costs the sweep one bound (up to 160 s) instead of one per call, and no timed-out or failed write is reported converged. No config key, env var or flag; the 120 s bound is unchanged.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_store/git_outcome.py`: `GitTimedOut`, `raise_if_watchdog_timeout`.\n- `src/rebar/_store/ensures.py`: sweep stop; marker comment; docstrings.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/_init_ensures.py`: `_git` stops on a timeout; write results checked.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/init.py`: untrack unit checks; `_pin_auto_maintenance`; docstring.\n- `docs/migrations.md`.\n- `tests/unit/test_ensure_git_timeout.py` (new).\n\n## Testing\nIn the new module, with the units' git seam stubbed to return a code-124 watchdog result:\n- Fails on the current tree: every `config` call timed out, `_gc_config_unit` raises `GitTimedOut` after exactly 1 git call (today: 5 calls, `changed`).\n- `run_ensures` with the stub: `gc-config` is `failed` naming the command, no later unit is invoked (recording stub), `.ensure-applied` omits gc-config and every later unit.\n- Recovery: stub removed, the next `run_ensures` has no `failed` outcome and `.ensure-applied` lists every registry id.\n- `_untrack_runtime_markers_unit` with `ls-files` timed out raises `GitTimedOut`, not `ok`.\n- Real git with a held `config.lock`: `_gc_config_unit` and `_merge_ours_unit` each return `failed` with \"could not lock config file\".\n- `_pin_auto_maintenance` with the stub returns after 1 git call and logs a warning.\n- A unit raising `ValueError` is still recorded `failed` and the sweep continues.\n- Re-run the shim reproduction (2 s bound, tracker `git config` stalled during the sweep) on the fixed tree: 1 stalled call instead of 7, `gc-config` `failed`, no `changed`. Result recorded on this ticket.\n- Existing tests pass; `make verify` before push.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] The worst-case wall time of one `run_git_write` call, retries included, is measured and stated.\n- [x] The blocked-`git config` condition is reproduced, or a stated experiment shows why it cannot be, with the evidence recorded on this ticket.\n- [x] A fix (or a justified close as environmental, with evidence) lands, with a regression test if a code defect is found."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795411518065310001,"uuid":"87acb097-fbd1-40b6-a160-4cb9110aad95"}